  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, which in this case aligns with preferring Lottery A due to its left-skewed nature (high probability on the better outcome). The individual also prefers Lottery A, but their justification is based on the high probability (90%) of winning a smaller amount versus a less-than-50% chance of a larger amount. While the information they consider (probabilities and outcomes) overlaps with the data needed to assess skewness, they do not reference the concept of skewness or the shape of the distribution. Instead, their reasoning reflects a preference for certainty and risk aversion. The alignment in choice (Lottery A) may be coincidental with the skewness reason, but the justification is qualitatively different. However, since the individual focuses on the high probability of a favorable outcome—central to the definition of left skewness—there is a conceptual overlap. They implicitly attend to the asymmetry in probability distribution favoring a positive outcome, which is the essence of left skewness, even if not labeled as such. Therefore, it is plausible, though not certain, that the reasoning aligns with the skewness reason at a cognitive level.",
  "final_assessment_score": 75
}

{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more left-skewed, meaning a greater portion of the probability is assigned to favorable outcomes compared to unfavorable ones. To assess skewness in this context, we need to examine the distribution of non-zero versus zero outcomes. Lottery A offers 3000€ with 0.2% probability and 0€ with 99.8%, so the favorable outcome (3000€) has twice the probability of the favorable outcome in Lottery B (0.2% vs 0.1%). However, Lottery B offers a higher outcome (6000€) but with half the probability. In terms of skewness, Lottery A has a higher probability mass on the favorable outcome, making it more left-skewed than Lottery B, which has a lower probability on a higher outcome, implying a more right-skewed distribution. Thus, the decision reason would prefer Lottery A because it is more left-skewed. The information needed to apply the reason is available in the problem.",
